  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-35)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta =-35.$\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-35)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$ We do this by adding integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get our desired angle:\n\n$$\n-{35}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={325}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{35}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({325}^{∘}).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266661/assets/74d116436f69dea77aaf08bda4949e824dfec7a38127577a81362f407b7db75e.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta ={325}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{325}^{∘}={35}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The cosine function is positive $(+)$ in the fourth quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{35}^{∘})=+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({35}^{∘})\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{35}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({35}^{∘}).\n$$",
